  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe introduce a novel approach based on I-divergence minimization between the function h and its shifts. This differs significantly from previous methods that focused on correlation or simple thresholding. The central idea is that optimal solutions often exhibit self-similarity under shifts, which can be captured by minimizing the I-divergence from the target distribution. We first formalize the problem in continuous terms and then discretize it. The method uses a constrained optimization with barrier functions to maintain feasibility and employs a multi-stage improvement procedure to refine candidates.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nContinuous domain: h(x): [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1.\nObjective: min_max_{k\u2208\u211d} \u222b\u03bbI_div(|\u03bb| ln(\u03bb)) + (1-\u03bb) ln(1/\u03bb) d\u03bb over \u03bb = h(x)h(x+k)\nwhere \u03bb(x,k) = h(x)h(x+k) and I_div(\u03bb) is the binary I-divergence.\nDiscrete approximation: Use step function with n=425 equally spaced points.\nOptimizer interface:\n- Objective function: f(x) = sum_{k} exp(\u03b1*(I_div(x) - max_I_div(x))) with \u03b1=1000\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, sum(h) = 222.5\n- Gradient calculation using adjoint methods\n3. Implementation procedure:\na) Validate input size (n=425)\nb) Select initial strategy (typically 16 blocks per phase)\nc) Phase 1: Initial block decomposition over [0,2] (fixed boundaries)\n   - Set boundaries = [0, 0.25, 0.5, ..., 1.75, 2]\n   - Initialize block values uniformly\n   - Project to satisfy integral constraint\n   - Compute rough score\nd) Phase 2: Local search with block swapping\n   - For each block, try flipping or adjusting boundaries\n   - Use grid-based local search\n   - Calculate score for top candidates\ne) Phase 3: Crossover with best block from previous generation (16 blocks)\n   - Maintain archive of top performers\n   - Adaptive cooling schedule\nf) Phase 4: Smooth-max optimization (quadratic barrier)\n   - Use barrier parameters \u03bc to enforce constraints\n   - Implementation requires automatic differentiation\ng) Return best candidate along with its raw score\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n- Boundary density: 1_equally spaced [default], 2_high_sparsity (20 blocks), 3_annealing schedule [adaptive]\n- Update rules: stochastic_block_swap, adaptive_blowout, entropy_weighted\n- Archive: population size typically 50 (if time permits)\n- Local search: 3 parameters \u2192 distance metrics, cooling rates, mutation factors\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\nPhase 1: 300s \u2192 10-block analysis\nPhase 2: 500s \u2192 local search\nPhase 3: 300s \u2192 crossover\nPhase 4: 200s \u2192 final polish\nCheck time using `time.time() - t0`\nSafe guards: \n- Fallback to initial solution using hard constraints\n- Warm start from best found in previous half-cycle\n- Gradient clipping at 1e7\n6. Validation and return contract:\na) Check: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, sum(h) \u2248 222.5 \u00b1 1e-3\nb) I-divergence calculation from scratch:\n   raw_score = max_k \u222b... d\u03bb * dx (exact)\nc) Use `np.isfinite` and `np.ndim` checks\nd) Final candidate must be re-evaluated in original mode\ne) Sanitized output:\n   h_values = final_block_values @ interpolate_heights\n   n_points = 425\nf) When boundaries vary, use L2-projection to n=425",
